\subsection{Proof of Corollary \ref{cor:1}}\label{pf:cor:1}

We condition on the event \eqref{event:EE}, which happens with probability $1-\delta\pi^2/6$. By Lemma \ref{lem:4}, we know that, for $t\geq \T_1$,
\begin{equation*}
f(\xb_t) - f(\ttxb) \leq (1 - \phi)^{t-\T_1}(f(\xb_{\T_1}) - f(\ttxb)) \leq (1 - \phi)^{t-\T_1}(f(\xb_0) - f(\ttxb)).
\end{equation*}
By Lemma \ref{lem:pre:1}, to have $\xb_t \in \N_\nu$, it suffices to have $\xb_t \in \bar{\N}_{\nu^2/3}$. Thus, we let
\begin{equation}\label{equ:new}
(1 - \phi)^{t-\T_1}(f(\xb_0) - f(\ttxb)) \leq \frac{\nu^2\lambda_{\min}^3}{3L^2} \Longleftarrow t-\T_1 \geq \frac{\log\rbr{\frac{3L^2(f(\xb_0) - f(\ttxb))}{\nu^2\lambda_{\min}^3}}}{\log\rbr{\frac{1}{1-\phi}}}\Longleftarrow t \geq \T_1 + \T_2,
\end{equation}
where $\T_2$ is defined in \eqref{T2} and the implication uses the fact that $\log(1/(1-\phi))\geq \phi$. Furthermore, since $f(\xb_t)$ is always decreasing, we know after $\T_1+T_2$ iterations $\xb_t$ stays in $\bar{\N}_{\nu^2/3}$, and hence stays in $\N_{\nu}$ (cf. Lemma \ref{lem:pre:1}). This completes the proof.

